germany has made another pledge to  support ukraine with heavy weaponry  announcing today the supply of seven  self-propelled armoured howitzers now  the artillery system which resembles a  tank has a reported range of up to 40  kilometers the german military will also  offer training to ukrainian troops  berlin has been under pressure to do  more to help ukraine defend itself  against the russian invasion last month  the government agreed to export  german-made anti-aircraft tanks  and for more on this i'm joined now by  dw's chief political correspondent  melinda crane melinda welcome germany is  saying it will deliver more heavy  weapons to ukraine what more can you  tell us about that  that's right this announcement was made  this morning by defense minister  lambrecht who is currently visiting  german soldiers deployed in slovakia  where they're helping to reinforce  nato's eastern flank and these seven  howitzers are exactly the kind of heavy  weapons that ukraine has been asking  germany to provide minister lambert said  that germany will also offer training  that has been a source of uncertainty in  the past and one of the reasons that  germany has been hesitant about  delivering heavy weapons because of  concern about how training could be  interpreted by russia whether that could  be seen essentially as making germany a  kind of party to the conflict the german  government has as you said repeatedly  been accused by the leadership in kiev  of not doing enough to help defend it  against the russian invasion and until  just 10 days ago berlin had been quite  reluctant to provide heavy weapons but  chancellor schultz does appear to have  changed course under pressure from  allies and even from his own coalition  partners and has also pledged to export  german-made gaypod armored anti-aircraft  vehicles to ukraine so definitely we are  seeing movement now on the part of  berlin  if we can talk about relations between  berlin and kiev they have been tense  recently after excuse me germany's  president frank walter steinmeier was  told that he was not welcome in ukraine  but according to  chancellor olaf schultz it does seem  that the diploma diplomatic hatchet has  now been buried let's just take a listen  in  the german president and the ukrainian  president spoke very carefully to each  other  that's a good thing  i spoke in detail to the german  president about it beforehand and  afterwards  and the result of the conversation  is that the foreign minister will soon  be in ukraine  so it seems the diplomatic spat has been  smoothed out yet  still isn't going to ukraine why is that  well schultz has in fact in the past  made no secret of his irritation over  the fact that the german president  essentially was disinvited  from visiting ukraine there had been a  very short-term  planned visit when he would have gone  with his polish counterpart and ukraine  rather abruptly said that the german  president would not be welcome and uh  chancellor schultz has spoken quite  openly about his uh irritation over that  but uh  it's difficult to speculate about the  emotional  side of chancellor schultz's remarks  because in fact he is very restrained as  a communicator he comes from northern  germany and he's known for  understatement if you listen to what  president steinmeier said yesterday  after his telephone conversation with  president zielenski of ukraine it was  far more effusive and in fact zolensky  did invite the president as well as the  german government to visit so possibly  chancellor schultz's hesitation is now  simply a protocol issue because normally  it is the president who makes such  official state visits uh first  now all of schultz was quite heavily  criticized  for his reaction to the snub the  ukrainian ambassador to germany accused  charlotte of sulking how much public  backing here in germany does olaf  schwartz have for the way that he's  handling this crisis in ukraine  public is very divided particularly on  whether germany should in fact meet  ukraine's request to send heavy weapons  the  number of or the share of uh polls that  actually support that of ranges between  45 to 56  of those surveyed so essentially the  country is split and that is  particularly true of members of the  chancellor's own party the social  democrats they have a long tradition of  pacifism of believing that  good commercial relations with other  countries will ensure peace and  stability and therefore this is a  difficult move for the german government  to make and we heard chancellor schultz  on the traditional union labor union  rally on may first saying to er to his  listeners in the face of cat calls that  radical pacifism has now become obsolete  so clearly he is trying to take the  public along with him but at the moment  the country is split for fear of being  drawn into this conflict  melinda crane dw's chief political  correspondent thank you so much  and we're going to cross now to dw's  chief political editor michela kufner  who is traveling with the german defense  minister in slovakia michigan what can  you tell us about the minister's visit  yeah it's here in slovakia that german  defense minister christine lamprey made  the announcement that germany will  deliver seven howitzers bringing up the  number to 12 that will go towards  ukraine here behind me she's been  inspecting patriot defense systems  that's also another cooperation with the  netherlands with the dutch defense  minister here as well both countries  together will do the training on these  howitzers in germany  it will be ukrainian soldiers coming  here who will be trained and that  training is due to start as early as  next week with the howitzers being  delivered over the coming weeks we're  not getting a more precise timeline but  quite clearly uh germany willing to hand  over more heavy weapons always together  with its allies within the nato context  and also potentially willing to deliver  more that's what we're hearing we know  that there is a growing list in the  german chancellery of what can be done  so we expect this only to be the latest  step in potential future heavy weapons  deliveries  dws michaela kufna in slovakia thank you
 

